      "id": "aHR0cHM6Ly9ncmVlbnJvYWQuY29tL2Jsb2cvYmFjay10by1zY2hvb2wtd2hhdC1jaGFuZ2VzLWZvci1idXMtYW5kLWNvYWNoLW9wZXJhdG9ycw",
      "title": "Back to School: What Changes for Bus and Coach Operators",
      "location": null,
      "url": "https://greenroad.com/blog/back-to-school-what-changes-for-bus-and-coach-operators/",
      "description": "Back to School: Bus & Coach Safety Guide for Operators Products AskMila™️ – AI assistant GreenRoad Digital Edge™ Software Solution GreenRoad Drive™ App GreenRoad Edge™ Hardware Solution GreenRoad Central™ Safety platform Videosense™ Pro Video Telematics Industries Passenger Transport Oil & Gas, Mining Utilities Delivery Food & Beverages Construction Services About Us Company Overview Investors Offices Partners Careers Resources Blogs Articles Customer Stories Brochures and Ebooks Webinars Events Contact Us Products AskMila™️ – AI assistant GreenRoad Digital Edge™ Software Solution GreenRoad Drive™ App GreenRoad Edge™ Hardware Solution GreenRoad Central™ Safety platform Videosense™ Pro Video Telematics Industries Passenger Transport Oil & Gas, Mining Utilities Delivery Food & Beverages Construction Services About Us Company Overview Investors Offices Partners Careers Resources Blogs Articles Customer Stories Brochures and Ebooks Webinars Events Contact Us Category: Driver Safety Back to School: What Changes for Bus and Coach Operators By Noa Yitzhak - Head of Marketing When term starts, your network changes overnight. Loadings jump, stops that were quiet all August fill up, and the routes your drivers know best stop behaving the way they did last week. For operators running home-to-school contracts, dedicated school services, or scheduled routes that fill with students from the first morning of term, this is the point in the year when exposure and liability rise together. More passengers boarding at more stops. Tighter timings on the same running board. Children moving around the vehicle and around the kerb, doing things adults do not do. Safe journeys do not happen by accident. Over 90% of bus accidents are caused by driver behaviour, which means the biggest lever an operator has is the one already behind the wheel. The start of term is the moment to reinforce habits, spot the routes that are drifting, and make sure every driver is ready for the road as it is now, not as it was in the summer. Your driver is doing four jobs at once A bus or coach driver on a school service is managing the road ahead, the passengers behind them , the door, and the clock. All at the same time. The pressure points are specific: Boarding and alighting. School stops load fast and unevenly. Children push forward, stand before the vehicle stops, and step off into a kerb crowded with other children. The saloon. Standing passengers , bags in the aisle, noise, and movement while the vehicle is going. The driver is watching that on a mirror while reading the road. The door and the nearside. Younger children are hard to see around a large vehicle, especially in the moments right after they get off. The timetable. A service running late puts pressure on exactly the manoeuvres that matter most: approach speed to the stop, pulling away, and the turn out of a school entrance. Everything a car driver faces in a school zone, your driver faces too. On top of the four things above. The risk is not confined to the school gate Term traffic changes the whole network, not just the streets around schools. Journey times shift. Congestion builds earlier. Queues appear in places that were clear a fortnight ago. Drivers meet more cyclists, more pedestrians and more sudden stops on routes that have nothing to do with a school. Which is why looking at individual incidents is not enough. One harsh braking event near a school entrance is one driver reacting to one unpredictable moment. That is noise. The same event, at the same place, at the same time, across several drivers, is not noise. That is a route problem , and it is fixable before it becomes an incident. One event is noise. A pattern is a route problem. The data you already collect will tell you, in the first two weeks of term: Which routes have seen harsh braking, acceleration or cornering climb since term started Where drivers repeatedly meet the same hazard Which times of day carry the extra risk Which drivers would benefit from coaching now rather than after an incident Where the schedule itself is producing the unsafe behaviour The point is not to collect more data. It is to use what is already there. If four drivers brake hard at the same school entrance, the answer is probably not four coaching conversations. It is a route review, a timetable adjustment, or one targeted briefing for everyone who works that approach. Feedback while it still matters No driver can predict what a child, a cyclist or a distracted parent will do next. What a driver can control is the time and space available to react: a safe approach speed, a longer following distance, no unnecessary acceleration between stops. In-cab feedback reinforces those habits during the journey. An alert in the moment gives the driver the chance to adjust now. An incident report gives them the chance to think about it next week. Paired with constructive coaching back at the depot, in-cab feedback helps drivers see their own risk patterns and know what to do differently tomorrow morning. Week one of term: who owns what Technology helps. The decisions are still human, and in the first fortnight they sit in three places. The safety team watches for change, not for absolutes. Compare this week against the last week of the summer timetable on the same routes. Rising event rates on a specific route or a specific time band are the signal worth acting on. The scheduler owns the running times. If a route cannot be driven safely to its timetable now that loadings have doubled, that is a planning decision, not a driver failing. Fix it in week one, before it becomes the normal way that route gets driven. The depot owns the briefing. Drivers new to a school route need to know where the pinch points are before they meet them: which entrance has poor visibility, which stop crowds the kerb, which turn needs a second look. The point When children are back on the roads, everyone needs to pay closer attention. For operators, that attentio",
